1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,259
This is a professional telephoto lens.

2
00:00:03,433 --> 00:00:07,287
It's long, it's heavy, and it's packed with thick glass,

3
00:00:07,415 --> 00:00:10,364
because that's what the laws of optics demand.

4
00:00:10,503 --> 00:00:12,198
And this is a phone.

5
00:00:12,477 --> 00:00:14,938
It's less than nine millimetres thick.

6
00:00:15,438 --> 00:00:17,865
By the rules that shaped that big lens,

7
00:00:17,946 --> 00:00:23,066
a camera this thin shouldn't be able to zoom, shouldn't hold steady in your hand,

8
00:00:23,205 --> 00:00:25,713
and definitely shouldn't see in the dark.

9
00:00:25,991 --> 00:00:28,046
And yet it does all three.

10
00:00:28,418 --> 00:00:31,773
So today, we're going to follow a single ray of light.

11
00:00:32,005 --> 00:00:35,894
It leaves a streetlamp, slips through a sapphire window,

12
00:00:36,069 --> 00:00:39,842
gets folded around corners inside a block of glass,

13
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:43,754
lands on a chip of silicon that floats on magnets,

14
00:00:43,789 --> 00:00:46,936
and comes out the other side as a finished photo.

15
00:00:47,377 --> 00:00:50,094
The whole trip takes a fraction of a second.

16
00:00:50,465 --> 00:00:52,241
Let's slow it down.

17
00:00:54,270 --> 00:00:55,524
Start with the problem.

18
00:00:55,652 --> 00:01:00,597
To magnify a distant subject, a lens needs a long focal length.

19
00:01:00,725 --> 00:01:05,601
Put simply, the light needs room to travel after it passes through the glass,

20
00:01:05,683 --> 00:01:07,564
before it comes into focus.

21
00:01:07,842 --> 00:01:09,920
More zoom, more distance.

22
00:01:10,153 --> 00:01:14,797
That's why sports photographers carry lenses the size of a forearm.

23
00:01:15,029 --> 00:01:20,056
A phone's telephoto camera needs more than a centimetre and a half of travel.

24
00:01:20,427 --> 00:01:23,341
Stack that on top of the lens and the sensor,

25
00:01:23,411 --> 00:01:26,395
and it simply doesn't fit inside the phone.

26
00:01:26,627 --> 00:01:30,911
So engineers stopped trying to go deep, and went sideways instead.

27
00:01:31,282 --> 00:01:36,286
Beneath the lens sits a single slab of glass, called a tetraprism.

28
00:01:36,658 --> 00:01:37,714
Watch the ray.

29
00:01:37,947 --> 00:01:42,579
It drops in through the top, and hits an angled, mirror-coated face.

30
00:01:43,113 --> 00:01:43,636
One.

31
00:01:43,845 --> 00:01:46,910
It shoots across and glances off the top surface,

32
00:01:46,944 --> 00:01:50,172
where it's trapped by total internal reflection.

33
00:01:50,625 --> 00:01:51,182
Two.

34
00:01:51,530 --> 00:01:52,540
Off the bottom.

35
00:01:52,889 --> 00:01:53,516
Three.

36
00:01:53,725 --> 00:01:55,663
Off a second angled mirror.

37
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:56,685
Four.

38
00:01:57,092 --> 00:02:01,051
Only then does it drop out of the glass, and onto the sensor.

39
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:03,164
Think of it like this.

40
00:02:03,953 --> 00:02:08,945
You need a runner to cover a long hallway, but all you have is a closet.

41
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:15,110
So you line the closet with mirrors, and let the runner zigzag from wall to wall,

42
00:02:15,285 --> 00:02:17,572
until they've covered the full distance.

43
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:20,555
Same journey, a fraction of the space.

44
00:02:20,997 --> 00:02:23,667
The light has no idea it was folded.

45
00:02:23,795 --> 00:02:27,858
It arrives having travelled much farther than the phone is thick.

46
00:02:28,357 --> 00:02:29,530
One more trick.

47
00:02:29,901 --> 00:02:34,418
To focus, something has to move, and in a camera this cramped,

48
00:02:34,476 --> 00:02:38,156
there's no room to slide heavy glass back and forth.

49
00:02:38,945 --> 00:02:43,705
So the sensor itself moves, nudged by tiny electromagnets,

50
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:47,990
in steps measured in microns, thousandths of a millimetre,

51
00:02:48,164 --> 00:02:51,345
until the image snaps sharp.

52
00:02:53,377 --> 00:02:56,477
Now there's a second problem, and it's you.

53
00:02:56,790 --> 00:02:59,577
Your hands are never perfectly still.

54
00:02:59,751 --> 00:03:03,153
Your pulse, your breathing, the press of your thumb,

55
00:03:03,524 --> 00:03:08,087
all of it makes the phone tremble by tiny fractions of a degree.

56
00:03:08,586 --> 00:03:11,221
On a wide shot, you'd never notice.

57
00:03:11,512 --> 00:03:16,701
But zoom in, and that tremble is magnified along with everything else.

58
00:03:17,247 --> 00:03:20,893
During the exposure, the image smears across the sensor,

59
00:03:20,985 --> 00:03:23,667
and a point of light becomes a scribble.

60
00:03:24,410 --> 00:03:28,288
Big cameras fix this by moving a group of lens elements.

61
00:03:28,485 --> 00:03:30,958
Early phone cameras did the same thing.

62
00:03:31,190 --> 00:03:35,254
But glass is heavy, and heavy things are slow to move.

63
00:03:35,532 --> 00:03:40,316
So here, the lens stays put, and the lightest part moves instead:

64
00:03:40,548 --> 00:03:41,512
the sensor.

65
00:03:41,883 --> 00:03:44,716
The silicon chip isn't bolted down.

66
00:03:44,844 --> 00:03:50,707
It hangs on fine springs, surrounded by copper coils and small permanent magnets.

67
00:03:51,206 --> 00:03:54,933
Run a current through a coil, and it pushes against its magnet,

68
00:03:55,061 --> 00:03:58,486
sliding the sensor sideways, or up and down.

69
00:03:59,066 --> 00:04:02,421
A gyroscope feels the phone tilt one way.

70
00:04:02,793 --> 00:04:04,430
Before the blur can form,

71
00:04:04,523 --> 00:04:09,074
the coils shove the sensor so that it follows the image exactly.

72
00:04:09,446 --> 00:04:10,804
The body shakes.

73
00:04:10,932 --> 00:04:15,053
The picture, as far as the sensor is concerned, never moves.

74
00:04:15,251 --> 00:04:17,340
And this isn't one correction.

75
00:04:17,537 --> 00:04:18,512
It's a loop:

76
00:04:18,768 --> 00:04:22,982
measure, move, check the position, and do it again,

77
00:04:23,110 --> 00:04:28,555
thousands of times every second, far faster than any tremor in your hand.

78
00:04:29,019 --> 00:04:32,305
It's the same idea as noise-cancelling headphones:

79
00:04:32,491 --> 00:04:36,601
sense the disturbance, and produce its exact opposite.

80
00:04:37,530 --> 00:04:39,039
The payoff is time.

81
00:04:39,271 --> 00:04:41,303
With the image pinned in place,

82
00:04:41,372 --> 00:04:44,937
the shutter can stay open several times longer without blur,

83
00:04:45,064 --> 00:04:48,060
and a longer exposure means more light.

84
00:04:48,338 --> 00:04:54,445
And as we're about to see, for a sensor this small, light is everything.

85
00:04:56,481 --> 00:04:57,619
Let's go closer.

86
00:04:57,897 --> 00:04:58,873
Much closer.

87
00:04:59,314 --> 00:05:04,341
The surface of the sensor looks like a mirror, but zoom in a few thousand times,

88
00:05:04,434 --> 00:05:06,489
and it turns into a city grid:

89
00:05:06,802 --> 00:05:11,493
tens of millions of identical cells, each about a micron across.

90
00:05:11,772 --> 00:05:14,442
A human hair would cover dozens of them.

91
00:05:14,755 --> 00:05:19,167
Each cell is a photodiode, a tiny well of silicon.

92
00:05:19,364 --> 00:05:23,440
When a particle of light, a photon, strikes the silicon,

93
00:05:23,637 --> 00:05:27,468
it knocks an electron loose, and the well holds on to it.

94
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:30,521
More photons, more electrons.

95
00:05:30,951 --> 00:05:32,681
That's all a pixel is:

96
00:05:32,832 --> 00:05:33,691
a counter.

97
00:05:33,958 --> 00:05:35,096
But there's a catch.

98
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:38,927
Silicon can count light, but it can't tell what colour it was.

99
00:05:39,252 --> 00:05:43,815
A red photon and a blue photon leave the same electron behind.

100
00:05:44,128 --> 00:05:48,680
So every well gets a filter on top, red, green, or blue,

101
00:05:48,808 --> 00:05:51,072
that only lets its own colour through.

102
00:05:51,350 --> 00:05:54,288
And above that, a tiny dome of a lens,

103
00:05:54,415 --> 00:05:59,547
that funnels light down into the well instead of letting it spill onto the wiring.

104
00:06:00,232 --> 00:06:01,323
Look at the pattern.

105
00:06:01,521 --> 00:06:07,117
In every block of four, there's one red, one blue, and two green.

106
00:06:07,489 --> 00:06:09,230
That isn't a mistake.

107
00:06:09,602 --> 00:06:12,144
Your eyes are most sensitive to green light,

108
00:06:12,283 --> 00:06:15,476
and it's where you pick up most of the brightness and detail,

109
00:06:15,650 --> 00:06:18,425
so the sensor spends half its pixels there.

110
00:06:18,925 --> 00:06:20,446
Here's the way to picture it.

111
00:06:20,887 --> 00:06:25,090
Imagine millions of buckets, set out in a rainstorm.

112
00:06:25,461 --> 00:06:27,632
Every photon is a raindrop.

113
00:06:28,073 --> 00:06:32,334
When the shutter closes, you measure how much water is in each bucket.

114
00:06:32,833 --> 00:06:35,550
In dim light, only a few drops fall,

115
00:06:35,725 --> 00:06:38,755
and it's down to luck which buckets they land in.

116
00:06:39,033 --> 00:06:41,715
One gets five, its neighbour gets two.

117
00:06:42,087 --> 00:06:45,848
That randomness is the grain you see in night photos.

118
00:06:46,046 --> 00:06:48,983
In bright light, you get the opposite problem.

119
00:06:49,355 --> 00:06:53,952
The bucket fills to the brim and overflows, and once it's full,

120
00:06:54,033 --> 00:06:56,645
it can't tell bright from blinding.

121
00:06:57,144 --> 00:06:59,083
That's a blown-out sky.

122
00:06:59,525 --> 00:07:04,807
When the exposure ends, each bucket is measured, and turned into a number.

123
00:07:05,179 --> 00:07:06,804
Millions of numbers.

124
00:07:07,083 --> 00:07:09,904
And that's what leaves the sensor.

125
00:07:11,940 --> 00:07:14,506
So what does the sensor actually hand over?

126
00:07:14,703 --> 00:07:15,562
Not a photo.

127
00:07:15,655 --> 00:07:16,700
Not even close.

128
00:07:16,828 --> 00:07:18,500
This is the raw frame.

129
00:07:18,604 --> 00:07:19,510
It's dark.

130
00:07:19,579 --> 00:07:20,508
It's grainy.

131
00:07:20,613 --> 00:07:25,304
And it has no colour at all, just a brightness reading for every pixel,

132
00:07:25,478 --> 00:07:30,644
laid out in a checkerboard, where each square only knows about one of red, green,

133
00:07:30,714 --> 00:07:31,654
or blue.

134
00:07:32,153 --> 00:07:37,018
Two thirds of the colour information in the final picture was never recorded.

135
00:07:37,390 --> 00:07:38,968
It has to be worked out.

136
00:07:39,410 --> 00:07:42,719
That job belongs to the image signal processor,

137
00:07:42,823 --> 00:07:46,457
a block of the phone's main chip that does nothing else.

138
00:07:47,142 --> 00:07:53,005
First, it pulls the mosaic apart into three sheets, red, green, and blue,

139
00:07:53,202 --> 00:07:55,060
each one full of holes.

140
00:07:55,501 --> 00:07:57,243
Then it fills the holes.

141
00:07:57,742 --> 00:08:00,239
For a pixel that only measured green,

142
00:08:00,366 --> 00:08:03,292
it looks at the red neighbours on either side,

143
00:08:03,350 --> 00:08:06,171
and estimates the red it would have seen.

144
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:10,049
It's an educated guess, but a careful one:

145
00:08:10,246 --> 00:08:14,472
the processor looks for edges, and avoids blending across them,

146
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:19,326
so that a sharp line stays sharp, instead of growing coloured fringes.

147
00:08:19,813 --> 00:08:22,600
This step is called demosaicing,

148
00:08:22,728 --> 00:08:26,466
and it happens for every one of those millions of pixels.

149
00:08:26,954 --> 00:08:28,347
Next, colour.

150
00:08:28,834 --> 00:08:33,838
The sensor has no idea that the streetlamp is orange and the shade is blue.

151
00:08:34,326 --> 00:08:37,147
Your brain corrects for that automatically.

152
00:08:37,704 --> 00:08:41,408
A white shirt looks white to you almost anywhere.

153
00:08:41,837 --> 00:08:46,377
So the processor has to do the same, estimating the colour of the light,

154
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:50,800
and scaling the three channels until neutral things look neutral.

155
00:08:51,357 --> 00:08:52,971
That's white balance.

156
00:08:53,865 --> 00:08:55,548
Then, the grain.

157
00:08:56,024 --> 00:08:59,577
The processor compares each pixel with its surroundings,

158
00:08:59,844 --> 00:09:03,292
works out what's real detail and what's random speckle,

159
00:09:03,466 --> 00:09:08,017
and smooths away the speckle, while trying to leave the texture alone.

160
00:09:08,574 --> 00:09:12,046
Then tone, contrast, and sharpening.

161
00:09:12,522 --> 00:09:15,297
And only now does it look like a photograph.

162
00:09:15,645 --> 00:09:20,718
Every one of these steps runs on every frame, thirty or sixty times a second,

163
00:09:20,881 --> 00:09:22,843
just to draw the viewfinder.

164
00:09:23,609 --> 00:09:27,127
But at night, even this isn't enough.

165
00:09:29,176 --> 00:09:30,186
Here's the limit.

166
00:09:30,685 --> 00:09:35,120
A sensor the size of a fingernail simply can't collect much light in one go.

167
00:09:35,805 --> 00:09:40,043
Leave the shutter open longer, and anything that moves turns to blur.

168
00:09:40,322 --> 00:09:45,499
Turn up the amplification, and you amplify the noise right along with the picture.

169
00:09:46,289 --> 00:09:48,994
One exposure is always a compromise.

170
00:09:49,365 --> 00:09:51,037
So the phone doesn't take one.

171
00:09:51,536 --> 00:09:56,819
From the moment you open the camera, it's already shooting, frame after frame,

172
00:09:57,051 --> 00:10:02,856
into a small rolling buffer, keeping the last few and throwing the oldest away.

173
00:10:03,646 --> 00:10:09,311
Many of them are short exposures, that freeze motion and protect the highlights.

174
00:10:09,625 --> 00:10:13,445
When you tap the shutter, you're not really taking a picture.

175
00:10:13,723 --> 00:10:16,428
You're telling the phone which moment you meant.

176
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:19,435
It keeps the frames around that instant,

177
00:10:19,505 --> 00:10:23,951
and adds a longer exposure to dig detail out of the shadows.

178
00:10:24,265 --> 00:10:28,978
In very dark scenes, it keeps collecting for a second or more.

179
00:10:29,420 --> 00:10:34,795
Now it has a stack of frames of the same scene, and none of them quite line up,

180
00:10:34,993 --> 00:10:38,197
because your hand moved, and so did the world.

181
00:10:38,569 --> 00:10:43,642
So the chip aligns them, shifting and warping each one, patch by patch,

182
00:10:43,735 --> 00:10:46,730
until every edge sits on top of itself.

183
00:10:47,172 --> 00:10:50,875
Anything that moved too much to match gets left out.

184
00:10:51,073 --> 00:10:52,350
Then they're merged.

185
00:10:52,791 --> 00:10:58,642
The scene is the same in every frame, but the noise is random, different every time.

186
00:10:59,142 --> 00:11:04,657
Average the frames, and the picture reinforces itself, while the noise cancels out.

187
00:11:05,342 --> 00:11:08,291
Nine frames, about a third of the noise.

188
00:11:08,569 --> 00:11:11,727
Machine learning models, running on the neural engine,

189
00:11:11,855 --> 00:11:15,048
decide pixel by pixel which frame to trust:

190
00:11:15,245 --> 00:11:20,609
the short ones for highlights, the long one for shadows, the sharpest for texture.

191
00:11:21,108 --> 00:11:26,577
The result is a photo that no single exposure from this sensor could have produced.

192
00:11:26,890 --> 00:11:28,597
It's not faking the scene.

193
00:11:28,794 --> 00:11:33,914
It's measuring it many times, and keeping the best of every measurement.

194
00:11:35,963 --> 00:11:37,089
So pull back.

195
00:11:37,461 --> 00:11:41,408
Out of the silicon wells, where photons became electrons.

196
00:11:41,849 --> 00:11:43,614
Up through the colour filters.

197
00:11:43,846 --> 00:11:48,003
Back through the glass that folded a telephoto lens into a sliver,

198
00:11:48,177 --> 00:11:50,673
past the sensor floating on its magnets.

199
00:11:50,987 --> 00:11:54,586
Out through the sapphire, and back to the thing in your hand.

200
00:11:55,027 --> 00:11:55,886
One tap.

201
00:11:56,165 --> 00:12:01,784
A beam folded four times, a sensor steadied thousands of times a second,

202
00:12:01,912 --> 00:12:07,043
tens of millions of buckets counted, and billions of calculations,

203
00:12:07,240 --> 00:12:09,887
all in about the time it takes you to blink.

204
00:12:10,119 --> 00:12:11,675
That's one camera.

205
00:12:11,780 --> 00:12:15,019
There's a lot more hardware hiding in plain sight.

206
00:12:15,251 --> 00:12:16,935
So tell me in the comments:

207
00:12:17,167 --> 00:12:19,152
what should we take apart next?

208
00:12:19,349 --> 00:12:21,091
This is Deep Teardown.

209
00:12:21,265 --> 00:12:23,425
I'll see you inside the next one.
